## Part 3: Adding a database header

You've got a blank canvas. Now it's time to start designing your site.

Like most newsroom developers, you don't have to start from scratch. As we discussed in [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/), most teams maintain a library of pre-made, ready-to-serve components that they can pull into projects.

They are often collected and documented in a playbook shared among developers. One common tool for building and browsing these component libraries is called [Storybook](https://storybook.js.org/). It lets you see every component in isolation, experiment with its props, and copy the usage pattern directly into your project.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/storybook-js.png"
  alt="The Storybook homepage showing its hero section with logos of supported frameworks like React, Vue, and Angular"
  chromeTitle="Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://storybook.js.org/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Our class has just such a site, packaged alongside our components in the [static-site template](https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template). You can view it at [palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/).

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/storybook-homepage.png"
  alt="The component library Storybook for the CUNY static site template, showing all available components in the left sidebar"
  chromeTitle="Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The [`DatabaseHeader`](https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template/blob/main/src/lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte) component is the first piece you want to use today. It renders a full-width hero banner designed for database pages, with props for a headline, a description, a byline, and a date.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/storybook-database-header.png"
  alt="Storybook documentation page for the DatabaseHeader component, showing a live preview of the header with its available props listed in a controls table below"
  chromeTitle="DatabaseHeader – Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-databaseheader--docs"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

It's intended to mimic the design pattern used by ProPublica and many other news sites to frame their database projects, which often lack compelling photography, with a bold top.

### Import the component

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Your script block currently logs the data to the console. Add an import for `DatabaseHeader` at the top:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
<script>
  import DatabaseHeader from '$lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  console.log(`Loaded ${data.violations.length} buildings`);
  console.log("First record:", data.violations[0]);
</script>
```

Add the component below the closing `</script>` tag, beginning with just the headline and description.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="10-14"}
<script>
  import DatabaseHeader from '$lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  console.log(`Loaded ${data.violations.length} buildings`);
  console.log("First record:", data.violations[0]);
</script>

<DatabaseHeader
  kicker="Data Explorer"
  headline="Tainted Home Tracker"
  description="The Bronx buildings with the most lead paint violations"
/>
```

Save and look at your browser. A pale banner appears across the top of the page with the headline and deck set in large type. That's the component's default style, just like we saw on the Storybook page.

<Screenshot
